an cover multiple points in a column are vertical lines (cover all points in that column). Horizontal lines cover at most one point per column (since they have constant y). Slope -1 lines also cover at most one point per column (since x+y = constant => for a given x, y is determined). So to cover multiple points in a column, we need a vertical line. So we need a vertical line x=n-2. So indeed, one of the non-sunny lines must be vertical x=n-2.\n\nNow consider column x=n-1 (size 2). Points: (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). If we have a vertical line x=n-1, that would be a second vertical line. But we have only n-2 non-sunny lines total, and we already used one vertical line x=n-2. Could we also use vertical line x=n-1? Possibly, but we need to check if we can avoid using a vertical line x=n-1. The solution says: If we do not use vertical line x=n-1, both its points must be covered by sunny lines, i.e., each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so we need two sunny lines to cover both points. Since we have k <= 2, we could have k=2 and use both sunny lines to cover those two points. But we also need to cover column n-2's three points: we have vertical line x=n-2 covering all three points. So we have used one vertical line and two sunny lines covering the two points in column n-1. That uses up all k=2 sunny lines. Then we need to cover the remaining points, including (n,1). The point (n,1) is in column n, which has only one point. It cannot be covered by a vertical line (since we have no vertical line x=n). It could be covered by a sunny line, but we have no sunny lines left (k=2 used). It could be covered by a non-sunny line: horizontal line y=1 or slope -1 line x+y= n+1? Let's see: (n,1) lies on horizontal line y=1, which is non-sunny. So we could use a horizontal line y=1 as a non-sunny line. But we have only n-2 non-sunny lines total, and we have used one vertical line x=n-2. We could also use horizontal line y=1. That would be a second non-sunny line. So we have