s (2,5) not in set, (3,6) not in set. So only (1,4). For (2,1) => c=-1 => line y = x-1 includes (1,0) not allowed, (3,2) not in set (but (3,2) is covered already by line x+2y=7). So not helpful. For (2,4) => c=2 => line y = x+2 includes (1,-? ) no, (3,5) not in set. So only (2,4). For (4,1) => c=-3 => line y = x-3 includes (1,-2) not allowed, (2,-1) not allowed, (3,0) not allowed. So only (4,1). For (4,2) => c=-2 => line y = x-2 includes (2,0) not allowed, (3,1) which is covered already. So not helpful.\n\nThus slope 1 lines cannot cover three of the remaining points.\n\nCheck slope -2 lines: y = -2x + b. For (1,2): b = 4 => line y = -2x +4 includes (2,0) not allowed, (3,-2) not allowed. So only (1,2). For (1,4): b = 6 => line includes (2,2) which is covered already, (3,0) not allowed. So only (1,4). For (2,1): b = 5 => line includes (1,3) covered already, (3,-1) not allowed. So only (2,1). For (2,4): b = 8 => line includes (1,6) not in set, (3,2) covered already. So only (2,4). For (4,1): b = 9 => line includes (3,3) covered already, (2,5) not in set. So only (4,1). For (4,2): b = 10 => line includes (3,4) not in set, (2,6) not in set. So only (4,2). So slope -2 lines cannot cover three of the remaining points.\n\nCheck slope -1/2 lines: x + 2y = d. For (1,2): d = 5 => line includes (3,1) covered already, (5,0) not allowed. So only (1,2). For (1,4): d = 9 => line includes (3,3) covered, (5,2) not in set (5,2 sum 7 >6). So only (1,4). For (2,1): d = 4 => line includes (2,1) only? Actually x+2y=4 includes (2,1) and (0,2) not allowed. So only (2,1). For (2,4): d = 10 => line includes (2,4) and (4,3) not in set. So only (2,4). For (4,1): d = 6 => line includes (4,1) and (2,2) covered. So only (4,1). For (4,2): d = 8 => line includes (4,2) and (2,3) covered. So only (4,2). So slope -1/2 lines cannot cover three of the remaining points.\n\nThus none of the three slopes can cover three of the remaining points. Could there be other slopes that can cover three points among the remaining set? Let's try to find any line (not parallel to axes or slope -1) that passes through three of these points. The remaining points are (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2). Let's see if any three are collinear with a slope not 0,∞,-1.\n\nCheck pairs:\n\n- (1,2) and (2,1): slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1 => slope -1, not allowed (sunny lines cannot be slope -1). So not allowed.\n\n- (1,2) and (4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, allowed (not -1,0,∞). Does any third point lie on that line? Equation: y - 2 = (-1/3)(x - 1) => y = 2 - (x-1)/3 = (6 - (x-1))/3 = (7 - x)/3. For integer points, need (7 - x) divisible by 3. For x=4 => y = (7-4)/3 = 1 => (4,1) is on line. Already used. For x=7 => y=0 not allowed. So only those two points. So no third point.\n\n- (1,2) and (4,2): slope = (2-2)/(4-1) = 0 => horizontal, not sunny.\n\n- (1,2) and (2,4): slope = (4-2)/(2-1) = 2 => allowed. Equation: y - 2 = 2(x - 1) => y = 2x. For x=2 => y=4 (point (2,4)). For x=3 => y=6 not in T_5. So only those two points.\n\n- (1,2) and (1,4): vertical line x=1, not sunny.\n\n- (1,4) and (2,1): slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3 => allowed. Equation: y - 4 = -3(x - 1) => y = -3x + 7. For x=2 => y=1 (point (2,1)). For x=3 => y=-2 not allowed. So line passes through (1,4) and (2,1). Only two points.\n\n- (1,4) and (2,4): horizontal, not sunny.\n\n- (1,4) and (4,1): slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -1 => slope -1, not sunny.\n\n- (1,4) and (4,2): slope = (2-4)/(4-1) = -2/3 => allowed. Equation: y - 4 = (-2/3)(x - 1) => y = 4 - (2/3)(x-1) = (12 - 2(x-1))/3 = (14 - 2x)/3. For x=4 => y = (14 - 8)/3 = 6/3 = 2 => (4,2). For x=7 => y = (14 - 14)/3 = 0 => not allowed. So only those two points.\n\n- (2,1) and (2,4): vertical, not sunny.\n\n- (2,1) and (4,1): horizontal, not sunny.\n\n- (2,1) and (4,2): slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 => allowed. Equation: y - 1 = (1/2)(x - 2) => y = (x/2) = x/2? Actually y = 1 + (x-2)/2 = (x/2) + 0? Let's compute: y = 1 + (x-2)/2 = (2 + x - 2)/2 = x/2. So line y = x/2. For x=2 => y=1 (point (2,1)). For x=4 => y=2 (point (4,2)). For x=6 => y=3 not in T_5. So only those two points.\n\n- (2,4) and (4,1): slope = (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2 => allowed. Equation: y - 4 = (-3/2)(x - 2) => y = 4 - (3/2)(x-2) = 4 - (3x/2) + 3 = 7 - (3x/2). For x=4 => y = 7 - 6 = 1 => (4,1). For x=2 => y = 7 - 3 = 4 => (2,4). For x=0 => y = 7 => not allowed. So only those two points.\n\n- (2,4) and (4,2): slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -1 => not sunny.\n\n- (4,1) and (4,2): vertical, not sunny.\n\nThus no line among the remaining points passes through three points. So we cannot cover the remaining six points with two sunny lines each covering at most three points. Since each line can cover at most three points, we would need each to cover exactly three points, but we cannot find such lines. So k=5 is impossible.\n\nThus the solution's claim that only k=0,1,3 are possible seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to verify for all odd k > 3, the impossibility holds. The solution's argument is that the only lines that achieve the bound are the three lines (10) (the three slopes). However, we need to verify that indeed for any odd k, the only sunny lines that can contain floor((k+1)/2) points in T_k are those three lines (with slopes 1, -2, -1/2) and specific intercepts that align with the triangle. If that is correct, then we cannot have more than three lines achieving the bound, so k must be ≤3. But we need to check if there could be other lines that also achieve the bound for larger k, perhaps with different intercepts, but still covering floor((k+1)/2) points each, but not necessarily the same three lines. The solution claims that any other line would have |p+q|≥3 or |p+q|=1 with q≥2, leading to a strictly smaller bound. However, they earlier argued that for |p+q|=1, the bound is also floor((k+1)/2). Let's examine that.\n\nIn Section 2, they derived bound (2.4): floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). They then considered case |p+q|=1: then p+q = ±1. They argued that p+q=1 forces q≥2 (otherwise p=0 which would give a horizontal line). Because x = x0 + q t must stay in interval [1,k], the number of admissible t is at most floor(k/q)+1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). So they claim bound holds for |p+q|=1 as well. So lines with |p+q|=1 also achieve the bound? Actually they claim the bound holds for all lines, but the bound may be strict for some lines. The bound is an upper bound; some lines may achieve it, some may not. They claim that the bound is sharp (see §4). In §4 they say \"Equation (2.1) with |p+q|=2 gives three possibilities: p=q=1 (slope 1), p=-2,q=1 (slope -2), p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2).\" They ignore the case |p+q|=1. They claim that any other choice would have |p+q|≥3 or |p+q|=1 with q≥2, and then (2.4) gives a strictly smaller upper bound. But earlier they said for |p+q|=1, the bound is also floor((k+1)/2). Let's examine more carefully.\n\nBound (2.4) is floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). For |p+q|=1, floor((k-1)/1)+1 = (k-1)+1 = k. So bound says number of points ≤ k. But floor((k+1)/2) is about (k+1)/2. For k≥2, k > floor((k+1)/2). So the bound (2.4) is not the same as floor((k+1)/2) for |p+q|=1. Wait, they derived (2.4) as floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). Actually they wrote:\n\n\"the number of admissible integers t is at most floor((k+1-2)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k-1)/2)+1 = floor((k+1)/2).\" Let's check: they derived number of admissible t ≤ floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1. Then they claim this is ≤ floor((k-1)/2)+1 = floor((k+1)/2). This inequality holds because |p+q| ≥ 2? Actually they assume |p+q| ≥ 2? Let's see: they earlier said \"Because the line is sunny, p+q ≠ 0; therefore the sum changes by at least |p+q| ≥ 1 when t varies by one.\" So they only know |p+q| ≥ 1. Then they claim floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k-1)/2)+1. This inequality holds only if |p+q| ≥ 2. If |p+q| = 1, then floor((k-1)/1)+1 = k, which is > floor((k-1)/2)+1 for k>2. So the inequality fails for |p+q|=1. So they implicitly assume |p+q| ≥ 2. But they later discuss |p+q|=1 separately. So the bound (2.5) that each sunny line contains at most floor((k+1)/2) points holds for all sunny lines? Let's verify.\n\nIf |p+q|=1, then p+q = ±1. For p+q = 1, p = 1 - q. Since q>0 integer, p integer. For p+q = 1, we have p = 1 - q. If q=1, p=0 (horizontal line, not sunny). So q≥2, p negative. For p+q = -1, p = -1 - q, q>0 => p negative. So in both cases, |p+q|=1, but q≥2. Then the line equation is p x - q y = c. The sum x+y changes by p+q = ±1 each step. So the number of integer points in T_k is at most floor((k-1)/1)+1 = k. But we need to consider the constraint that x must stay within [1,k] and y must stay within [1,k] and x+y ≤ k+1. The bound k is larger than floor((k+1)/2) for k≥2. So the bound floor((k+1)/2) is not necessarily valid for lines with |p+q|=1? Actually they claim that for |p+q|=1, the bound is also floor((k+1)/2) because of the extra constraint that q≥2 restricts the number of points. Let's examine.\n\nTake k=5, consider line with p+q=1, q=2, p=-1. So line: -x - 2y = c => x + 2y = -c = d. That's the same as slope -1/2 line we considered earlier (x+2y = d). For k=5, we saw that lines of this type can have at most 2 points? Actually we saw that x+2y = d can have at most 3 points (d=7 gave 3 points). So for q=2, we can achieve 3 points, which equals floor((5+1)/2)=3. So bound holds.\n\nNow consider q=3, p=-2 (p+q=1). Then line: -2x - 3y = c => 2x + 3y = -c = d. Let's see how many points can be on this line in T_5. Compute 2x+3y = d. For points in T_5, x≥1,y≥1,x+y ≤6. Let's enumerate possible values of d for points:\n\nList points and compute 2x+3y:\n\n(1,1): 2+3=5\n(1,2): 2+6=8\n(1,3): 2+9=11\n(1,4): 2+12=14\n(1,5): 2+15=17\n\n(2,1): 4+3=7\n(2,2): 4+6=10\n(2,3): 4+9=13\n(2,4): 4+12=16\n\n(3,1): 6+3=9\n(3,2): 6+6=12\n(3,3): 6+9=15\n\n(4,1): 8+3=11\n(4,2): 8+6=14\n\n(5,1): 10+3=13\n\nNow group by d:\n\nd=5: (1,1)\nd=7: (2,1)\nd=8: (1,2)\nd=9: (3,1)\nd=10: (2,2)\nd=11: (1,3),(4,1)\nd=12: (3,2)\nd=13: (2,3),(5,1)\nd=14: (1,4),(4,2)\nd=15: (3,3)\nd=16: (2,4)\nd=17: (1,5)\n\nSo each d yields at most 2 points. So lines with p+q=1 and q=3 can have at most 2 points. So bound floor((k+1)/2)=3 is not achieved. So indeed only lines with q=2 (i.e., slope -1/2) can achieve the bound. Similarly, lines with p+q=-1 and q=2 (i.e., slope 1/2? Actually p+q=-1 => p = -1 - q. For q=2 => p = -3. So line -3x - 2y = c => 3x + 2y = -c = d. That line may also have at most 2 points? Let's check. But anyway.\n\nThus the only lines achieving the bound are those with |p+q|=2 (p=q=1, p=-2,q=1, p=-1,q=2). So the solution's claim that only three lines achieve the bound seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to verify the final conclusion: only k=0,1,3 are possible. Let's test for n=5, k=3 works as they gave construction. For n=5, k=0 works. For n=5, k=1 works. For n=5, k=3 works. For n=5, k=5? That would require covering T_5 with 5 sunny lines. We argued it's impossible. So answer seems correct.\n\nBut we need to verify for n=3, k=3 works? For n=3, we need 3 lines, exactly 3 sunny lines. The construction for k=3 uses n-3 vertical lines (i.e., 0 vertical lines) and three sunny lines covering T_3. T_3 is points with x+y ≤4: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). The three lines given cover those points. So for n=3, we have exactly 3 lines, all sunny, covering all required points. So k=3 works for n=3.\n\nCheck for n=4, k=3 works: Use n-3=1 vertical line x=1, and three sunny lines covering T_3 (points with x+y ≤4). But after using vertical line x=1, we cover points with x=1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3). The remaining points are (2,1),(2,2),(3,1). That's exactly T_2? Wait T_3 is defined as points with x+y ≤ k+1 = 4. But after covering x=1, the remaining points are those with x≥2 and x+y ≤4. Those are (2,1),(2,2),(3,1). That's exactly the set covered by the three lines? Actually the three lines for T_3 are y=x (covers (2,2)), y=-2x+5 (covers (2,1)), y=-0.5x+2.5 (covers (3,1)). So they cover the remaining points. So k=3 works for n=4.\n\nThus answer seems correct.\n\nNow we need to chec