ve exactly 2 sunny lines and 2 non-sunny lines. Let's try to replace vertical line x=1 with a sunny line L1 that covers some points in column 1, and replace horizontal line y=1 with a sunny line L2 that covers some points in row 1. But then we need to ensure all points are still covered.\n\nLet's try to design a configuration with exactly 2 sunny lines for n=4.\n\nWe need to cover 10 points with 4 lines. Each non-sunny line can cover many points: vertical lines can cover up to 4 points (column 1), horizontal lines up to 4 points (row 1), diagonal lines up to 4 points (the main diagonal). Sunny lines can cover at most one point per column, but can cover many columns (one point each). So a sunny line can cover up to n points (one per column). For n=4, a sunny line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So a sunny line can be as effective as a vertical/horizontal line in terms of number of points covered, but with the restriction that it cannot cover more than one point per column.\n\nThus we need to allocate coverage.\n\nWe can think of covering the points by a set of lines such that each point is on at least one line. This is like a covering problem.\n\nWe can try to use two sunny lines that each cover many points, and two non-sunny lines that cover the rest.\n\nOne approach: Use sunny line L1: line through (1,4) and (2,2) maybe? Let's see slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, which is not 0, infinite, -1, so sunny. L1 passes through (1,4) and (2,2). It also passes through (3,0) which is not in region, (4,-2) not in region. So L1 covers (1,4) and (2,2). It also passes through (3,0) which is not required. So L1 covers 2 points.\n\nSunny line L2: line through (3,1) and (4,1)? That's horizontal, not sunny. So not allowed. Maybe line through (3,1) and (2,3) slope = (3-1)/(2-3) = 2/(-1) = -2, sunny. L2 passes through (3,1) and (2,3). It also passes through (1,5) not needed, (4,-1) not needed. So L2 covers (3,1) and (2,3). So far we have covered (1,4),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1). Points l